WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.560
A galaxy far, far away almost had a stand-in:
our actual galaxy.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:11.300
In 2016, when he was still attached to direct
Star Wars Episode IX: The Rise of Skywalker,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.910
Colin Trevorrow told a crowd at the Sundance
Film Festival that he had designs on shooting

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.100
scenes for the flick in actual space.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.000
Now, before you suggest that Trevorrow must
have been...well, off his rocker to suggest

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.660
such a thing, it may be helpful to know that
it's not like no movie cameras have ever made

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.480
the journey to the final frontier.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:34.829
Most notably, the Warner Bros. documentary
Hubble 3D, which followed a group of astronauts

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:39.809
on a delicate mission to repair the Hubble
Space Telescope, used IMAX cameras to film

00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:41.240
space-based scenes.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.520
Of course, these were shot aboard the Space
Shuttle Atlantis and not just, you know, in

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.399
open space but the fact remains that movie
cameras did indeed leave our atmosphere to

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:55.579
shoot scenes that we Earthlings would later
watch with our butts planted firmly in movie

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:56.460
theater seats.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.659
Apparently hoping to add a degree of realism
to a series in which ancient orders of mystical

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:05.970
space knights fight each other with laser
swords, Trevorrow informed the crowd that

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.890
not only was he hoping to shoot in space,
but that he had actually submitted a request

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:16.320
to the International Space Station to haul
IMAX cameras on up there and start filming

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:16.820
scenes.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.580
Strangely, he had not heard anything in response.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:20.820
The director admitted:

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.460
"I asked the question, 'Is it possible for
us to shoot IMAX film plates in actual space

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.460
for Star Wars?'

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.300
I haven't gotten an answer yet, but they've
shot IMAX in space!"

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:36.050
While that is true, we hear that special effects
houses are doing really incredible things

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:41.480
these days, and that the starry void is fairly
easily rendered on screen with much less hassle

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.220
and expense than it would take to get a film
crew onto the ISS for a month or so.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.900
One could argue that Trevorrow had a strangely
literal sense of what it would take to make

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:55.090
a Star Wars movie, and that argument would
be bolstered by remarks he went on to make

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:59.720
about his intention to shoot Rise of Skywalker
on film, rather than digital.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:00.460
He said:

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.560
"The only place where I tend to not be able
to attach myself entirely to something shot

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.720
digitally is when it's a period film.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.660
There's something in my brain that goes, 'Well,
they didn't have video cameras then.'

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.520
Film tends to remind us of our memories, of
our childhoods, the way we used to see films.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.999
I could never shoot Star Wars on anything
but film because it's a period film.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.540
It happened a long time ago!"

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:28.260
OK, so we could spend the rest of the day
poking holes in virtually every word of that

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:33.140
statement, but suffice to say that A) we don't
remember seeing any film cameras depicted

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:39.220
in the Star Wars movies, ever, and B) if it
was absolute accuracy Trevorrow was going

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.440
for, he should have lobbied to shoot the entire
film as a hologram.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.420
"It's a frickin' hologram."

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:45.140
"Yesss."

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.940
Oddly, it would take more than a year and
a half after the Sundance panel for Trevorrow

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:55.139
to be fired from Rise of Skywalker and replaced
with Star Wars Episode VII: The Force Awakens

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.580
director J.J. Abrams.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.840
The usual "creative differences" were cited
as the reason for his departure, with nary

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.400
a mention of the director's "the hell with
it, let's shoot it in space" phase.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:10.540
Fortunately, Trevorrow had another blockbuster
franchise to fall back on: Jurassic World,

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.300
the first installment of which he directed.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:18.120
For sequel Jurassic World: Fallen Kingdom
and the as-yet untitled upcoming third installment,

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:22.780
Trevorrow relinquished the director's chair
in favor of co-writing the screenplays; we're

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.540
going to go ahead and guess that this may
be because he wouldn't quit pitching the idea

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.740
of shooting certain scenes in the actual Jurassic
period.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:34.320
We're poking a lot of fun here, but Trevorrow's
remarks do raise the question of whether any

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.180
Hollywood production has ever managed to shoot
footage in actual space.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:39.040
The answer?

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.100
No, but not for lack of trying.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.700
In fact, Christopher Nolan happened to be
sitting on the same panel when Trevorrow dropped

00:03:45.709 --> 00:03:50.079
his space bomb, and he revealed that he had
actually attempted the same gambit for his

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.220
2014 film Interstellar.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:56.740
In the intervening years, Nolan has pointed
out that for that excellent effort, he selected

00:03:56.749 --> 00:04:02.120
the same IMAX cameras that NASA had previously
used to capture real footage from low Earth

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:02.620
orbit.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.420
But as far as actual space making an appearance
in a Hollywood film, it's never happened,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.860
and it almost certainly will not anytime soon.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:15.900
Heck, all but the last few minutes of Gravity
took place in space; and if that utterly mind-blowing

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:20.480
flick was able to portray its setting convincingly
without launching Sandra Bullock and George

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.900
Clooney into the cosmos, then it's a safe
bet that future filmmakers can do the same.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:30.720
No, unless and until it becomes cheaper to
deploy space-based film crews than to simply

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:36.100
hire a reputable CGI house, we're not likely
to see any Hollywood features utilizing footage

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.460
shot beyond the sad confines of our little
planet.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:43.240
Somehow, we don't think Star Wars Episode
IX: The Rise of Skywalker will suffer for

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:47.180
the omission; we'll find out when the flick
hits the big screen on December 20.

